though that they play in terms of  their frequency and intensity  well there's actually fairly limited  evidence that climate change is making  storms like dudley eunice or franklin  more intense or more frequent what we do  know though is that climate change is  increasing the severity of the impacts  that are associated with those storms so  for example we see more intense rainfall  and snowfall in some cases as the  climate warms and also because we've got  rising sea levels it means that we get  more intense storm surges that can reach  further up the coast it means that if we  do get those intensive rainfall periods  then you can get more inland flooding as  rivers burst their banks and things like  this and the the effects of these storms  can be more keenly felt and navraj  what's your view on this then the  current situation that we're facing  we're seeing you know three storms in a  matter of a week  yeah i mean it's uh i think as was just  said i mean there are some physical  impacts such as sea level rise or ocean  acidification which can be attributed to  climatic climate change with uh with  anthropogenic climate change with a high  degree of confidence um but the sorts of  events we're seeing are it's much harder  to link the um any given emission to any  particular outcome  but there's a lot of work being done to  try and connect those dots um but i  think we're clearly quite not quite  there yet  and in terms of not being there quite  yet actually this actually looks as our  whole infrastructure and i'll come to  you there ella is there something that  we need to be doing to prepare for  storms like these because every time it  happens we're seeing the problems that  people are facing with the lack of power  and it goes on for ages for days  sometimes  absolutely and my heart really goes out  to anyone who's been really  impacted negatively by these storms i  think we need to increase the amount of  action and work that we're putting into  actually preparing for these sorts of  events because extreme events generally  not just storms but you know flooding  droughts heat waves all of these kinds  of extremes are going to become more  frequent and more intense when they do  occur as the climate warms this problem  isn't going away anywhere anytime soon  and we really need to do everything that  we can not only to mitigate to prevent  the problem becoming even worse but also  to really adapt to the changes that are  already inevitable  and navraj what's the solution here what  do we need to be doing in order to  prepare for storms like these  there's a there's a need for a much  greater policy focus on adaptation i  think ella put it quite nicely when she  talked about the difference between  mitigation and adaptation a lot of  policy and  in this space is really about reducing  our emissions we're now at the stage  where reducing we just can't reduce our  emissions quickly enough we are going to  be living with degree we are living and  will continue to live with a degree of  climate change we need to respond to  that and that is and there's a whole  raft of um actions which need to be  taken part of the pro the problem is  twofold here on the one hand adaptation  tends to be highly localized it's not  something that lo that central  government can do we can readily  undertake  riverbank strengthening or floodplain  protection and so on  the the flip side to that is that local  governments in this country and in most  other jurisdictions are not as not as  well funded or coordinated  as central government so there's both a  problem uh or a capacity problem and a  technical problem there  all right thank you both very much for  the moment  but now it's a hundred days would you  believe it time moved so quickly since  the nation's promise to take serious  steps to try to stop global temperatures  rising by more than 1.5 degrees there  were a lot of pledges that were made at  cop26 all eyes were here we're on the uk