alf degrees warming what we are  seeing though is a huge amount of very  granular details patient work taking  place  across a wide range of sectors so  yesterday united states  changed its licensing system for new gas  fossil gas pipelines that's a really big  step there's ongoing work about the  support of international um uh finance  public finance for uh fossil fuels and  uh coal in particular uh this is stuff  which was you know completely off the  agenda even as recently as two or three  years ago it's now happening or in some  cases already happened  it's all you know it's relatively low  salience it's not picked up um but it is  happening is it happening fast enough no  um could we have imagined that even this  would be happening recently not at all  so i'm i'm i'm very much a glass half  full on this issue  not all issues but this one  no rose thank you very much and this  leads me on to my next point there um  ella because part of the criticism that  came out of the cop 26 summits was that  there were just too many words and not  enough action as i said  so what should we be doing then to  monitor those pledges that those  governments made how do we monitor those  changes that they actually make  i think that's a really challenging  issue and it's something that is or has  at least been kind of lacking in the  historical uh trajectory just because  there isn't some kind of centralized  body necessarily that has the authority  to really hold nations to account and i  think in  the sense that we can all hold our  leaders to account  the public can play a really important  role um and we can continue to demand  that our leaders and everyone in power  whether that's from government to  business to  all the other kind of organizations  across society are making those sorts of  changes that we want to want to see to  create the society that we want to see  well climate and polar scientists dr  ella gilbert and senior lecturer in  climate law at edinburgh university  navrag singh gali grateful for your time  thank you  thank you  well do you join us tomorrow because  we'll be joined by hosts of climate  curious podcast nariyam pesha and the  executive director of energy and climate  policy think tank climate imperative  bruce niles  and that is it from me thank you very  much indeed for your company i hope you  can join me again tomorrow see you then  [Music]  you
 

